A||N||18||FieroJoe||Squirt gun exhaust!||||10||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||05-28-2001||11:02 PM||||Today I set out to try and find a few things that would be causing some idle problems.  The car had been sitting for 2 days, and it had rained all day and night for both of those days (the rain here is getting very annoying).<P>So, I start it up, pop the decklid, and start poking around.  Next thing I know, my entire leg is wet from the exhaust!  Now I know sometimes a little condensation will come out, but this was literally just spraying out and getting everything wet.  There was so much water!  I revved the engine and big gobs of water came out!  After about two or three minutes it was finally done.<P>So, is it possible that there was <I>that</I> much condensation in the exahaust system?  I know there's been a lot of rain and dampness, but jeez!<P>My exhaust is pretty leaky too, could that be a reason why so much condensation got into it?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||10
Z||000001||Vader||05-28-2001||11:21 PM||||Joe,<P>Yes, there CAN be that much condensation in there.  If you thing you're tired of the rain (it rained here all last week and the system is moving your way) at least it isn't January, when all that rain would have to be shoveled.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||09:37 AM||||the tail pipes do slope down to the muffler, so a driving rain on the back of the car can fill your muffler with water.<P>  check your dipstick though, for muddy looking oil, to make sure its not a blown head gasket that is leaking coolant into a piston.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||05-29-2001||10:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>check your dipstick though, for muddy looking oil, to make sure its not a blown head gasket that is leaking coolant into a piston.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Funny that you should say something like that...my exhaust spews out white smoke, and smells sorta sweet.  I have been told that this is coolant burning.  Wierd thing is though, the oil looks perfect (I just changed it and watched it drain, no coolant in it and it looked like normal oil).  The coolant in the overflow bottle looks sorta dark, but it is still green.  It could also be that the coolant is old.  I havent done a compression check yet.  That is on my list of things to do though.||||reg||1
Z||000004||85Josh||05-29-2001||03:27 PM||||Sounds like the head gasket or the head itself is blown to an exhaust passage. You can sometimes run forever this way, sometimes, it causes the kind of overheating problems squoach has been dealing with.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||03:44 PM||||a head gasket can blow in lots of different places and cause coolant in the piston and exhaust, coolant in the oil, oil in the coolant, and exhaust in the coolant - which will make your overflow bottle look like its boiling when the engine is hot and running.<BR>  <BR>  you might want to flush your coolant and replace it to see if it gets funny looking again - but if you can smell that glychol - alcohol fragrence in your exhaust its probally a head gasket or cracked head.<P>   Or maybe the previous owner just did the heads? when I pulled the heads on my dodge van about a quart of coolant spilled out as I pulled them off, and a lot of it went into the exhaust pipe (which was disconnected and hanging loose)<P>  when i got the engine all put back together and started it up, it was running great - after a minute I looked back and it looked like I had started a steam engine - tons of steam everywhere.<P>   For a second I thought I was gonna be sick - thought I had screwed up the head gaskets - then I remembered the coolant spilling into the exhaust pipe.<P>  once it all blew out it was fine. but that only took about ten minutes to clear out - so if you smell coolant in your exhaust all the time - its a bad sign.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||05-30-2001||07:38 AM||||Well, I hope it is only a blown head gasket and not a cracked head.  I dont have any overheating problems, thank god.  It has been running this way for over a year, so maybe it is only leaking into an exhaust passage and not into a cylinder.  But, if it wasnt leaking into a cylinder, wouldnt a compression check do pretty much nothing in determining whether it was blown or not?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bazooka||05-30-2001||08:47 AM||||Joe,<BR>You can pressure test the cooling system to see if you have any leak down. You can also test your head/valves with a 'Cylinder Leakage Test' to see if anything is escaping with both valves closed. Basically, you pressurize the combustion chamber at the TDC position for each cylinder and look for pressure loss with a gauge that is screwed into the spark plug opening. Do each cylinder individually. If you listen at various points like the radiator filler opening, PCV opening in valve cover, or down the carburetor, you can detect leakage with hissing sounds or garbles with this test. <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <BR>***********************************<BR>[QUOTE]Originally posted by FieroJoe:<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||05-30-2001||01:34 PM||||Joe,<BR>Do you have overheating problems? Is your cooling system full? <BR>If it was leaking enough to hose you down, I'm sure it would be low.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||05-30-2001||09:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Joe,<BR>Do you have overheating problems? Is your cooling system full? <BR>If it was leaking enough to hose you down, I'm sure it would be low.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No actually,  temp needle never gets up to the first mark on the gauge.  There are no driveability problems that I can think of related to this (I could always be wrong).<P>Since I got done with the restoration, I don't think I have needed to add any coolant.  The level is quite constant in the overflow bottle.  That's the thing that baffles me, I mean if there's coolant burning, you would think that I would have to keep adding some.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fierowrecker||05-30-2001||11:39 PM||||Hey Joe!<BR>Wait till the weather dries out a bit before getting worried...<BR>If it continues to spew, then worry about it!<BR>I would add a can of bars-leak before I do anything, and if it continued, then a tear down is in order...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fiero_24||06-03-2001||10:59 AM||||Check the Evaporative Fuel canister.  I have had thes go bad where the purge valve on it starts sucking Fuel instead of air.  Every Fiero I have ever bought gets a new one (autozone $34.99 special order).  A good way to check is by looking at the exhaust manifold(s) at night.  If they glow red the canister needs replacing, or unplug the hose that goes to the intake area and plug it until you can get a new canister.  I have had the squirting exhaust and it went away after I replaced the canister.  My newest Fiero (87 GT) has the same problem now, and I just ordered a new canister.<P>Craig||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroJoe||06-03-2001||06:01 PM||||A leaky vapor canister can cause excessive water in the exhaust or white exhuast smoke?<P>I have not had any driveability issues relating to the vapor canister, and the entire area around it is dry (isnt leaking any raw fuel out of it anywhere).  All the rubber lines for it are brand new also.||||reg||1
Z||000013||sqoach||06-03-2001||09:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 85Josh:<BR><B>Sounds like the head gasket or the head itself is blown to an exhaust passage. You can sometimes run forever this way, sometimes, it causes the kind of overheating problems squoach has been dealing with.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey, it's sqoach!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P><BR>Hehe, j/k I ain't mad. Um, have you actually had this occur? I sometimes get excessive smoke and water from my tailpipe. And of course I have that problem. No coolant in oil or vise versa. So, if a little leak was causing coolant to get into the exhaust thingamadoodle, and exhaust gases getting into the coolant system, that would cause my overheat and would explain the whitish colored smoke and the water. Interesting...<P>Oh, sorry to take away from your topic FieroJoe.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000014||DecadenceR||06-03-2001||10:02 PM||||It's weird this topic came up.<BR>Friday i atarted doing a valve job on my 84 Duke. When I took off the head saturday, the normal coolant spilled into the cyllinders. But I noticed somethign kinda weird. Greenish coloring around the valves burnt in.<BR>Turns out my head gasket was bad, and I had no clue. It just so happened I was pulling off the head anyway...<BR>but I was getting the same symptoms. White exhaust once in a while, no coolant in the oil, or oil in the coolant. <BR>So I took the dremel to the head and used that to get all of the old gasket off. Hopefully I'll be all set tommrow evening...||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroJoe||06-03-2001||10:19 PM||||I dont mind squoach, you've got more of a problem than I do!  Since its driving just fine right now, I might wait until this winter to take the head off and have a looksy.  Im very fortunate that mine isnt overheating like yours.||||reg||1
Z||000016||85Josh||06-03-2001||11:39 PM||||squoach, I haven't had these problems on a Fiero, but I have experienced them in several other vehicles, from 4-8 cyl. Intermittent overheating, often within minites of startup, loss of coolant, white smoke, and that sickly-sweet smell in the exhaust. My old Subaru wagon lasted for about 3k mi like this, my 78 Chevy truck w/350 V8, lasted 5k till I blew it drag racing.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000017||85Josh||06-03-2001||11:44 PM||||FieroJoe, have you solved your idle problem? My 85 Duke was running between 800-1000 RPM at idle, then I lost the timing gear. After replacing it, the idle settled down to a steady 900! Close examination of the old gear showed that a couple of the teeth had been missing for a significantly longer time than the three-tooth section that stopped the car, and I think that variable valve timing was causing the idle trouble. <P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000018||FieroJoe||06-04-2001||07:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 85Josh:<BR><B>FieroJoe, have you solved your idle problem? My 85 Duke was running between 800-1000 RPM at idle, then I lost the timing gear. After replacing it, the idle settled down to a steady 900! Close examination of the old gear showed that a couple of the teeth had been missing for a significantly longer time than the three-tooth section that stopped the car, and I think that variable valve timing was causing the idle trouble. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, I guess it could possibly be bad.  The engine has at least 150,000 miles on it.  I've read a little bit about how one would replace the timing gear, it doesnt seem too hard but it sounds time consuming.  Is there an easier check to see if the chain or the gear are bad?||||reg||1
